body:
I'm not sure if this is an issue but it has caused me a bit of a headache. Since the Validation portion of the framework is packaged separately I figured it couldn't hurt to let you guys know about it.

So in my Cake 3 application I have Debug Kit installed. Now that the Validation section is packaged separately I have included it in another project I am working on. In my application I have a model that stores a class name and in the entity I use it like this:

``` php
    class SomeEntity extends Entity {

        protected $_Instance;

        public function instance () {

            if (!$this->_Instance) {
                $class = $this->get('class');
                $this->_Instance = new $class();
            }

            return $this->_Instance;

        }

    }
```

On that instance class I have several validation recipes. Everything works fine until I add conditional validation. When I add a closure to the `on` key I get the following error: `Serialization of 'Closure' is not allowed`. It is triggered [here](https://github.com/cakephp/debug_kit/blob/3.0/src/Routing/Filter/DebugBarFilter.php#L189) on Debug Kit and ultimately traces back to [Cake\ORM\ResultSet::serialize()](https://github.com/cakephp/cakephp/blob/3.0/src/ORM/ResultSet.php#L231-L236). I understand that I am not using the validation suite in the traditional way which probably makes this issue a grey area as far as what you guys need to support but since it is packaged separately now I figured it was something you guys would like to be aware of.

I'm sure there is an easy solution to this but I couldn't immediately find it. I played around with the idea of using [PHP magic methods](http://php.net/manual/en/language.oop5.magic.php#language.oop5.magic.sleep) to control the information serialized or maybe just unsetting the instance variable before serialization. I was worried I would mess up some expected data. If this is the solution it might be helpful to have the `__sleep()` and `__wakeup()` methods on the base `Entity` class so they can be overloaded. Kind of an inherited event callback, I guess.

Anyway, just wanted to let you guys know. Thanks for all of your hard work. I really enjoy Cake 3!
